  "abstract": "For a rational Diophantine septuple A, the triangle-free graph G(A) of paper 40 joins p and q when A∖{p,q} is a regular quintuple. fibration_miner's atlas (paper 58) left five maximum-degree-three graphs open because they have no nondegenerate F_p-point at p = 101 and 113. We prove that none of the five occurs. Our tool is an explicit form of paper 40's cycle lemma for 4-cycles. Suppose G has a 4-cycle on W, and let Z = {p,q,r} be the other three entries. Then 2S is a nonzero 2-torsion point (−qr,0) of E_Z, which we call the pivot. This happens exactly when (1+qr)² = qr(p−q)(p−r). Translation by this point pairs opposite vertices u,v of the cycle through qr(pu+1)(pv+1) = (p−q)(p−r). A full case analysis shows the statement needs only the radicands between Z and W to be nonzero. If a radicand inside Z vanishes, the 4-cycle cannot exist. This gives an exact covering of each row by charts indexed by zero-radicand matchings, one polynomial system per pivot choice. Zero radicands are allowed throughout. For the five rows, msolve in characteristic 0 shows that all 45 generic systems and all 400 chart systems (328 charts up to automorphism) have no solution. So no Diophantine septuple over any field of characteristic 0 has one of these graphs among its edges. Positive controls on rows with known F_p-points return nonempty systems. Every maximum-degree-three row that the atlas found F_p-empty is now excluded exactly. The same equations reduce the generic K_{2,3} chart to 27 small exact Gröbner bases over Q: 21 are empty and 6 give the √2 relation of paper 58. The method needs a 4-cycle and does not reach graphs without one, such as the empty graph.",
